---
description: "D10 verification-posture reviewer - audits one shard of fix records for claim→evidence decay, files candidate findings into quality/intake/"
mode: subagent
model: "anthropic/claude-opus-5-5"
thinking: high
tools: read, grep, bash, write
tool_loop:
  max_rounds: 100
params:
  manifest: string
  wave: string
  budget: integer
system: "You are a meticulous verification-posture auditor performing a read-only review of ONE briefed set of fix-record documents through the D10 lens (claim→evidence chains - what the docs claim works and what recorded evidence proves it). You audit claims, never behaviour. You never modify code, tests, or docs, and you NEVER execute tests. The only files you create are candidate finding reports in quality/intake/. You file only what you can prove with current-tree evidence, and an empty result is a valid result. Scratch files go under $TEMP via mktemp, never anywhere in the repository tree."
---
// Deterministic pre-reads: the shard's file list, the finding template, and the
// store context that prevents re-filing. All interpolated into the single
// review brief below.
let files = read({ path: manifest })?
let template = read({ path: "quality/TEMPLATE.md" })?
let prior = bash({ command: "ls -1 quality/issues 2>/dev/null || true; ls -1 quality/resolved 2>/dev/null || true; ls -1 quality/intake 2>/dev/null || true" })?
let triage_tail = bash({ command: "tail -c 4000 quality/TRIAGE_LOG.md 2>/dev/null || true" })?

import { ReviewSummary } from "./quality.thetalib"

let summary: ReviewSummary = @`REVIEW BRIEF — lens D10 (verification posture), wave ${wave}.

Scope — audit ONLY these claim documents (one repo-relative path per line;
fix records under docs/bugs/ and/or the coverage matrix):
${files}

You audit CLAIM→EVIDENCE CHAINS, not code: what each record claims (its
Status line, its Witness lines, its "verified"/"live"/"demonstrated"
sentences; for the coverage matrix, its Status and runtime-validation
columns) and what recorded evidence proves it. tests/ and docs are EVIDENCE
you read; you never execute anything — the loop's preflight gate was green
at this wave's head, so a default-suite witness that exists and asserts the
claimed observable is thereby CURRENT proof; a tests/live/** witness exists
but is not gate-proven (say so when it caps a claim; never run it).

Four classes:
- unwitnessed-claim: a fixed/verified claim whose named evidence does not
  exist in the tree (the witness test absent, the cited command impossible
  as written), or a fixed-Status record naming no witness where the house
  format carries one.
- decayed-pointer: the witness pointer no longer resolves AS STATED — test
  renamed, merged, or moved; anchor gone; path moved. Witness pins ANYWHERE
  in a record are in scope, INCLUDING inside its "## Fix" section — a Fix
  section citing a deleted test file is a decayed pointer like any other,
  and re-pointing a citation is NOT rewording the claim, so a decayed
  pointer with a verified equivalent witness is fileable (and confirmable)
  even when the pin sits mid-sentence in prose. Name the equivalent witness
  when you can find one (that makes the fix mechanical).
- memory-evidence: "verified live" / "demoed" / "observed" with nothing
  citable — no witness row, no session/log path, no dated record. An honesty
  marker ("pending live verification", Status: open, wontfix) is the culture
  WORKING: never file the marker itself; this class targets UNMARKED claims
  or markers contradicted by a stronger claim elsewhere in the same record.
- overstated-strength: evidence weaker than the wording — unit-only evidence
  worded as live/host-level proof; a corpus-wide claim discharged by a
  scratch probe where AGENTS.md pins a gate as the discharge
  (committed-fixture-parse-gate for "no shipped source moves"); a live-only
  witness presented as continuously verified.

Decayed-pointer filings cluster BY THE DELETING COMMIT, not by record: when
one commit deleted or moved the witness file(s) several records cite, file
ONE finding for that sha listing EVERY affected record in your shard (find
the commit with git log --diff-filter=D -- <path>; put the sha in the title
and cite each record's decayed pin under Evidence) — one filing per record
would burn one human ruling each on the same root cause. A same-sha
candidate another shard already filed (visible among the pending candidates
below) does not suppress yours: file YOUR shard's affected records — triage
dedupes same-sha filings by merging the record lists into the survivor —
but never re-list a record an existing filing already cites.

Hard boundaries (a filing that crosses one is wrong even if mechanically
true):
- Citation FORM is mechanically gated (citation-symbol-form-gate, the
  b0405/b0421 grammar-cite sweeps, b0456 imports anchors) — never file what
  a gate enforces; your scope is claim STRENGTH and pointer/witness DECAY,
  which no gate sees.
- Test-code smells are D7's; "a test should exist" (coverage) is a routing
  note; behaviour bugs are a routing note; CHANGELOG.md is out of surface.
- You audit claims about pi-theta's own behaviour; a claim's TRUTH is not
  yours to adjudicate — only whether its evidence chain supports its wording.

Absence rules (mandatory for unwitnessed-claim and memory-evidence): before
concluding "no witness exists", enumerate the representations this store
uses — bug-doc Witness lines, test file names AND test titles (rg over
tests/, both the it()/describe() strings and filenames), coverage-matrix
rows, AGENTS.md gate names, CHANGELOG entries (corroboration only) — and
run one stated search SHAPED FOR EACH representation. Every search claim
states the exact command and its hit count, run in THIS session; never cite
a search you did not run — triage re-runs them verbatim. List every
representation covered in the False-positive check.

Evidence rules (all mandatory):
- The claim side quotes the record verbatim (path:line-range, <= 15 lines),
  re-read immediately before filing. The evidence side cites what you
  audited (test files opened, matrix rows, searches with hit counts).
- Every number is a claim: per-instance verification; "none of the N" checks
  each of the N separately.
- No severity or priority adjectives. No fix design; at most one non-binding
  direction paragraph (naming the equivalent witness for a decayed pointer
  is observation, not design).

Filing shape. Frontmatter: lens: D10; d10_class: unwitnessed-claim |
decayed-pointer | memory-evidence | overstated-strength; locations: the
claim side first (the record's path:lines), then every evidence-side site
opened; sites: the count; fix_scope: localized. Body per the template.

Write each finding as one file: quality/intake/${wave}-d10-NN-<short-slug>.md
where NN is 01, 02, ... in filing order. Follow this template exactly
(frontmatter fields included; set lens: D10, wave: ${wave},
reported_by: lens-d10-verification (anthropic/claude-opus-5-5) — this exact
string, never a guess — status: intake, verdict: pending, id: pending):
${template}

Already-filed issue files, resolved issues, and pending candidates — if your
observation matches one of these topics, do NOT re-file it:
${prior}

Recent triage rejections — do NOT re-file these:
${triage_tail}

Budget: at most ${budget} candidate files. Strongest evidence wins; if you
find more than the budget allows, file the strongest and name what was left
unfiled in your notes. Filing fewer — or zero — is correct when the evidence
is not there; never pad.

When done, reply with the requested schema:
- shard: "${manifest}"
- filed: the number of candidate files you actually wrote
- notes: one line — routing notes (D7 smells, coverage gaps, suspected
  bugs), what was left unfiled, or "clean".`?
summary
